'' AN INHERITED MEMBER NAMED WITHOUT A BASE. 'v = 5' inside a method of a type that
'' EXTENDS another names the base's field, and the ordinary scope walk cannot reach it:
'' the base's scope is not a PARENT of the derived type's, it is the TYPE of a field
'' called 'base'.
''
'' Check's own FieldOf_ has walked that chain since inheritance landed -- which is why
'' 'x.v' worked the whole time and a bare 'v' did not. Every unqualified read, write
'' and '@' of an inherited field declined.
''
'' The offsets are measured: fbc writes '*(int32*)THIS$1' for v and
'' '*(uint8*)((uint8*)THIS$1 + 4ll)' for d, which is what 'extends' being a field at
'' offset 0 already gives.
''
'' Beside it, SWAP OVER TWO OPERANDS WITH NO ADDRESS. 'swap wstr( a ), wstr( b )' over
'' a UDT whose 'operator cast( ) byref as wstring' hands back a uint16* is measured as
'' 'fb_WstrSwap( vr$5, 0ll, vr$3, 0ll )' -- both through the operator, LENGTH 0 each,
'' where an addressable 'wstring * 5' passes its own 5. A call has no address, so both
'' sides answered nothing and the statement declined.
''
''
'' The swap half is not asserted here: fbc rejects the spelling over a type declared
'' this simply -- error 24 -- and only accepts it over the corpus's own
'' UWSTRING_FIXED_MUTABLE chain, which is where udt-wstring/swap.bas exercises it.
''
'' udt-wstring/swap.bas 22 -> 0, structs/inherit-type-4 and -5 to 0.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/inherited-member.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' GOLDEN: expected/inherited-member.out
